  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nWhile playing around a bit with the enhanced `select` element semantics I realized that for the `option` element we'd need to observe changes to descendant elements to correctly handle this case:\n```html\n<p><select id=select>\n <div>\n  <option>2<div id=evil>3</div></option>\n </div>\n <div>\n  <option>1</option>\n </div>\n</select>\n\n<p><button onclick=evil.textContent='4'>Click</button>\n```\nWhen you hit \"Click\" the select is supposed to show 24, not 23. This might not be script-observable if you do everything lazily, but it's UI and reference-test observable and as such it probably needs to be defined.\n\nI'm not sure how we would go about defining this however as thus far we've avoided making the web platform itself rely on mutation observers (and it's unclear if that's what we want timing-wise). (I asked around and for WebKit using mutation observers for this is not something we're interested in.)\n\nAlso, to be sure, is this the only case with the enhanced `select` element that needs something like this?\n\ncc @josepharhar @domfarolino @rniwa @smaug---- ",
